  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1006 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nDry condition and pleasantly warm mid October warmth is forecast to \ncontinue starting the 2nd half of the week. Surface high pressure\nover the SE CONUS will provide a southerly flow across much of the \nDeep South. After a warm Wed with highs in the mid/upper 80s, lows \nWed night should cool to around 60s sheltered valleys to mid 60s near \nlarge bodies of water. A last period of unseasonably warm conditions\nare expected Thu, with highs rising into the low/mid 80s mountains to \naround 90 over parts of north central and NW Alabama. \n\nEarlier in the week, a system impacting parts of the Great Basin and\nIntermountain West (infused with moisture from ex tropical system \nRachel) should move east of the central Front Range Wed-Thu. This \nsystem will move rapidly to the ENE, and reform over the Great Lakes \non Fri. A cold front trailing south and west of this system should \nmove across the Tennessee Valley late Thu into Fri. Given that most\nof the dynamics associated with the parent low will stay well to our\nnorth, the usual area of showers accompanying the front will actually \nfade as they approach the Valley from the west. So except for winds \nbecoming NW on Fri, this frontal passage at the moment appears will \nbe a dry one. There were some timing differences between the model \ncamps with the front. The 18Z GFS was the quickest, bringing the\nboundary across before daybreak Fri, while the ECMWF and Canadian \ndid not bring it through until after midday Fri.\n\nRegardless, once the front passes temperatures will chill notably, \njust in time for the upcoming weekend. After lows Thu night mainly in\nthe low/mid 60s, highs on Fri will be a little cooler in the low/mid\n80s, as high pressure from the NE Pacific and Canada builds to the \nSE. Colder conditions will most certainly be felt Fri night, as lows \ntumble into the mid 40s to around 50. Stiff northerly winds of 10-20\nmph and higher gusts will make it chilly for those participating in \noutdoor activities. The cooler trend continues Sat, with highs mainly\nin the mid 60s. Normal high/low temperatures Sat are around 75/50. \nThe coldest air of the season should produce lows by Sun morning from\nthe mid/upper 30s to low/mid 40s (places near large bodies of water \nthat are still relatively warm). The lower end of this could bring a \nfrosting risk for the coldest spots, something we'll monitor. Cool \nconditions continue next Sun with highs from the mid 60s to lower \n70s.",
